As an overall comment on the idea: it will be a little difficult to work with this in practice. Interacting with an agent isn't a matter of invoking a skill, it's about providing the agent a task to perform that it uses its skills to complete. I think there's value in representing "when I need to use this skill, this is how long it takes", but keep in mind that clients don't dictate what skills an agent uses. The agent is the one who determines what skills are necessary to complete a task. So for a client to take this into account when planning, they are essentially making guesses as to what skills the agent might employ to accomplish a task.

In your proposed situation, where an agent has a menu of possible agents to choose from and wants to take latency into account, there's an implicit assumption around "skill comparability" which I think is tricky. Some agents will have higher-level skills that take on more or less work than other agents. You may be able to delegate more of a task to a single agent, or smaller parts of the overall task to different agents. Reasoning about latency in those cases would be pretty tough. At the very least, it's an interesting problem.

If an agent has a really strong handle on how long it takes to perform certain things, you could potentially ask it "estimate the time to complete the following task:", which could be expressed via an extension that introduces a new method (`message/estimateLatency`?). That estimation could take a long time to complete itself, so it's a bit fraught. Definitely keep looking into this, since I expect it would be very useful to others if you could come up with a solid solution to it.

Could the `google.protobuf.Any` ([definition](https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/blob/main/src/google/protobuf/any.proto)) type not play an elegant role here? 

```proto
message Any {
  // A URL/resource name that uniquely identifies the type of the serialized
  // protocol buffer message. This string must contain at least
  // one "/" character. The last segment of the URL's path must represent
  // the fully qualified name of the type (as in
  // `path/google.protobuf.Duration`). The name should be in a canonical form
  // (e.g., leading "." is not accepted).
  //
  // In practice, teams usually precompile into the binary all types that they
  // expect it to use in the context of Any. However, for URLs which use the
  // scheme `http`, `https`, or no scheme, one can optionally set up a type
  // server that maps type URLs to message definitions as follows:
  //
  // * If no scheme is provided, `https` is assumed.
  // * An HTTP GET on the URL must yield a [google.protobuf.Type][]
  //   value in binary format, or produce an error.
  // * Applications are allowed to cache lookup results based on the
  //   URL, or have them precompiled into a binary to avoid any
  //   lookup. Therefore, binary compatibility needs to be preserved
  //   on changes to types. (Use versioned type names to manage
  //   breaking changes.)
  //
  // Note: this functionality is not currently available in the official
  // protobuf release, and it is not used for type URLs beginning with
  // type.googleapis.com. As of May 2023, there are no widely used type server
  // implementations and no plans to implement one.
  //
  // Schemes other than `http`, `https` (or the empty scheme) might be
  // used with implementation specific semantics.
  //
  string type_url = 1;

  // Must be a valid serialized protocol buffer of the above specified type.
  bytes value = 2;
}
```

Inline with how Google uses common ([well-known](https://protobuf.dev/reference/protobuf/google.protobuf/) and [common](https://github.com/googleapis/api-common-protos)) protos to communicate well understood data structures (
